.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt TIMEOUT 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm timeout
.Nd run a command with a time limit
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f | -foreground
.Op Fl k Ar time | Fl -kill-after Ar time
.Op Fl p | -preserve-status
.Op Fl s Ar signal | Fl -signal Ar signal
.Op Fl v | -verbose
.Ar duration
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs
.Ar command
with its
.Ar arg Ns s
and, if it is still running after
.Ar duration ,
sends it a signal,
.Dv SIGTERM
unless another is given, followed by
.Dv SIGCONT
in case it was stopped.
.Pp
A
.Ar duration
or
.Ar time
is a number of seconds, fractions allowed, or of minutes, hours or days with
the suffix
.Ql m ,
.Ql h
or
.Ql d
.Po
.Ql s
for seconds may also be written
.Pc .
0 means no limit.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -foreground
Signal only
.Ar command ,
not the processes it starts, and leave it in
.Nm Ns 's
process group, so that it can read the terminal.
Otherwise
.Ar command
runs in a process group of its own, and the group is signalled.
.It Fl k Ar time , Fl -kill-after Ar time
If
.Ar command
is still running
.Ar time
after the first signal, send it
.Dv SIGKILL .
.It Fl p , Fl -preserve-status
Exit with
.Ar command Ns 's
status even when the time ran out.
.It Fl s Ar signal , Fl -signal Ar signal
Send
.Ar signal ,
a name (with or without
.Dq SIG ,
in any case) or number, when the time runs out.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Report each signal sent, and why, on the standard error.
.El
.Pp
A signal that would terminate
.Nm
itself is passed on to
.Ar command
instead, as if the time had run out; other signals are passed on.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 124
The time ran out (and
.Fl p
wasn't given).
.It 125
.Nm
itself failed, for example on a bad option or duration.
.It 126
.Ar command
was found but couldn't be run.
.It 127
.Ar command
wasn't found.
.El
.Pp
Otherwise, the exit status of
.Ar command ;
if it was killed by a signal,
.Nm
kills itself with the same signal (without a core dump), so that the shell
reports the same.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give a build ten minutes, and a minute more to clean up:
.Dl timeout -k 1m 10m make
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr nohup 1 ,
.Xr signal 3
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024
.Pq Dq POSIX.1 ;
.Fl v
and the long options are extensions.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.Fx 10.3 .
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fx
signals all of
.Ar command Ns 's
descendants by making
.Nm
their reaper; OxideBSD uses a process group instead, so a descendant that
leaves the group escapes, and without
.Fl f
a command that reads the terminal is stopped, as it is in the background.
